The shipped claim read "Runtime CLI subcommands do not shell out. All
subprocess invocations (curl, git, pip, rustup, gitleaks) happen only via
extras / build-time subcommands." That is FALSE as written. Measured with
strace -f -e trace=execve on a default `sketch` of a two-file repo:

  /usr/bin/git    x25  rev-parse HEAD, rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD,
                       config --get remote.origin.url
  gitleaks        x1   stdin --report-format json ...   <- a REAL secret scan
  rust-analyzer   x1   --version                        <- probe only

gitleaks is named in the claim as build-time-only and is not.

THE OWNER RULED (2026-08-11) TO DECLARE THE CAPABILITY, NOT REWORD THE CLAIM.
Rewording -- "except read-only repo inspection" -- was the cheap option and is
the one that launders: it weakens a published security claim with nothing
enforcing the carve-out's bounds, so the sentence becomes true by meaning less.

Three wrappers, not one, despite near-identical bodies -- mirroring the
existing cache_write / user_out_write / tmp_artifact_write split. The taint
machinery keys on the FUNCTION, so repo_inspect_git / repo_inspect_scan /
repo_inspect_probe let a future claim prohibit one activity while permitting
another. One wrapper would collapse "reads your git metadata" and "reads every
byte of your source" into a single indistinguishable permission.

BEHAVIOUR IS BYTE-IDENTICAL, WHICH IS THE POINT. The strace canary re-run
after the change: same three binaries, same counts. Nothing added, removed or
gated -- the capability was made VISIBLE to the analysis, not altered.

MEASURED:
  runtime-cli-no-subprocess  violated -> inconclusive
  1110 raw / 33 distinct unsanitized -> 0 unsanitized, with 390 flows still
  reaching the zone and passing a sanitizer on every route.
  Self-claim tally over this work: {violated 2, inconclusive 16} -> {inconclusive 18}.

ZERO VIOLATED IS NOT "PROVEN SAFE", and confirmed is still 0. Every verdict is
inconclusive because this repo contains bash/csharp/solidity with no taint
catalogue, so a negative cannot be confirmed. SECURITY.md regenerates to say
exactly that, publishes the enumerated repo-inspection surface, and records
that the previous wording was false.

build.rs is the load-bearing distinction and is pinned by a test:
rust-analyzer executes the analysed project's build.rs and proc macros when it
INDEXES, and the whole difference is --version versus an index request.
TestTheProbeDoesNotIndex fails if analysis-stats/scip/lsif/diagnostics ever
appear at that site.

One existing security test moved seam, intent unchanged:
TestNoCodeExecutionFromTargetRepo patched rf.subprocess.run, and
repo_fingerprint no longer imports subprocess. It now patches
repo_inspect_git, which is a TIGHTER seam -- the single declared chokepoint
for that module's process execution, so a git call added anywhere in it is
recorded by construction. Positive-controlled: the patched seam observes the
rev-parse invocation rather than silently recording nothing.

DEFERRED WITH AN EXPLICIT RE-EVALUATION TRIGGER: the probe shares the zone with
git and gitleaks, the weakest part of the grouping -- what unites the three is
what they CANNOT do (no host write, no network action, no execution of
repository code), not what they read. A separate tool_probe zone is deferred
because no claim today needs to prohibit the probe while permitting git. If
anything is ever added to this zone that reads repository CONTENT and acts on
it rather than reporting on it, split the zone first.

